      "heading": "Three ways to get a model, and what each really costs",
      "narration": "You have built a model, so now the honest question: in a real project, where does the model actually come from? There are three answers, and the choice is mostly an economics and constraints decision rather than a technical one. First, a hosted API. You send text to OpenAI or Anthropic or Google, you get text back, you pay per token. The advantages are real and worth stating plainly: these are the strongest models available, you operate no infrastructure, you get improvements without doing anything, and you can be running in an afternoon. The costs are equally real. You pay per call forever, your data goes to a third party, you have a hard dependency on a company whose pricing, availability and model behaviour can all change without your involvement, and a model you built a careful prompt against can be deprecated on a schedule you do not set. Second, open weights that you host yourself. Download Llama or Mistral or Qwen or Gemma, put it behind vLLM or Text Generation Inference on a GPU you rent or own, and serve it. Now the data never leaves your infrastructure, which for health, legal and financial work is often not a preference but a requirement. Your cost is per hour of GPU rather than per token, which changes the shape of the bill completely. Nothing is deprecated underneath you — the weights on your disk are the weights on your disk. And in exchange you are now operating a GPU service, with all that implies about capacity planning, monitoring, upgrades and someone being on call. Third, local. Ollama or llama.cpp running a quantized model on your own machine — a laptop, a workstation, a single card. It is free, it is completely private, it works with no network, and the models available are roughly a generation behind the frontier. That gap is smaller every year and it is currently real. This tier is excellent for development, for iterating on prompts without paying per attempt, for anything that must run offline, and for data that genuinely cannot leave the building. Now the economics, because this is the part that decides real projects. Per-token pricing is unbeatable at low volume: a thousand calls a day costs a few dollars and needs no infrastructure, and self-hosting would be strictly worse in every dimension. But per-token pricing scales linearly forever, while a rented GPU costs the same whether you send it one request an hour or a thousand a minute. So there is a crossover, and it arrives sooner than people expect — often in the low millions of tokens per day. The mistake teams make is not picking wrong at the start; the API is almost always right at the start. The mistake is not noticing when they have crossed over, and continuing to pay per token for a workload that has become steady and predictable.",
