      "heading": "\"Open-weight\" is not \"open-source\"",
      "narration": "This slide is short and it will save somebody a legal conversation. The phrase open source has a specific, long-established meaning, and most of the models described as open in this field do not meet it. Open weights means one thing: the parameters are downloadable and you can run them on your own hardware. That is genuinely valuable, and it is not the same as open source. What you typically do not get with open weights is the training data — nobody publishes the corpus, partly for competitive reasons and partly because its provenance would not survive scrutiny. You do not get the training code, or the exact data mixture, or the hyperparameter schedule. Which means you cannot reproduce the model, you cannot audit what went into it, and you cannot answer the question of why it behaves a particular way by looking at what it read. You have the artifact and not the recipe. And the licences are frequently not open-source licences at all. Llama's licence is a custom document with a monthly-active-user threshold above which you need a separate agreement, plus a list of prohibited uses. Several other model licences forbid using the model's outputs to train a competing model, which matters a great deal if distillation was part of your plan. None of these terms are unreasonable, and all of them will surprise a team that assumed open meant Apache or MIT. Read the licence before the model is load-bearing in a product, not after. There is a genuinely open-source category, and it is worth knowing it exists. Projects like OLMo from AI2 and Pythia from EleutherAI publish everything: the data, the training code, the intermediate checkpoints across the whole run. Those models are less capable than the frontier and vastly more useful if your question is scientific rather than commercial, because you can trace a behaviour back to the data that produced it and watch it emerge across checkpoints. If you are doing research on how these systems work rather than building a product with them, that is where to look. So the practical summary. Open weights buy you three specific things: reproducibility, in the sense that the model on your disk today is the model on your disk next year; privacy, because inference happens on your infrastructure; and freedom from deprecation, which is not a small thing when a hosted model you tuned prompts against gets retired with three months notice. They do not buy you the general freedom the word open suggests. Both of those facts should be in your head at the same time when you choose.",
