      "heading": "Tiers, and why the biggest model is the wrong default",
      "narration": "There is a default that costs teams a great deal of money, and it is: use the best model for everything. It feels safe. It is expensive and often slower for no benefit, and here is the reasoning. Frontier models cost somewhere between ten and fifty times per token what small models cost, and they are several times slower to respond. That premium is entirely justified on hard tasks — multi-step reasoning, subtle judgement, long-context synthesis, difficult code. It is entirely wasted on easy ones. And here is the uncomfortable observation about real systems: most of the traffic in a production LLM application is not hard. It is classifying a support ticket into one of six categories. Extracting four fields from a form. Deciding which of three tools to call. Rewriting a sentence into a house style. Summarizing a short paragraph. On those, a small model with a well-written prompt produces the same answer as the frontier model, at a twentieth of the cost and a third of the latency. Paying the premium there buys you nothing measurable at all. So the technique is routing, and it is the largest single cost lever in most LLM products. Send the easy majority to a small, fast model. Escalate to the expensive one when the small model's confidence is low, when the input is long or unusual, when the task is explicitly flagged as hard, or when a cheap validation step fails. In a typical application eighty to ninety percent of calls can be served by the small model with no quality difference a user could detect, which turns a cost curve from steep to nearly flat. And the same logic applies to latency: users notice a two-second response and do not notice a three-hundred-millisecond one, so routing improves the product as well as the bill. Finally, how to choose, because this is where leaderboards mislead people. A benchmark average tells you how a model does on a distribution of tasks that is not your distribution. It is weakly correlated with performance on your actual workload, and the correlation is weakest exactly where you care most — on the specialized, narrow thing your product does. So do the measurement yourself, and it is genuinely a day of work. Write down fifty to a hundred real examples of your task, with the answers you want. Run every candidate model against them with the same prompt. Compare quality, latency and cost per call side by side, in one table. You will frequently find that a model costing a twentieth as much is within a point or two of the leader on your specific task, and occasionally you will find that it is better, because your task rewards something the benchmark does not measure. That table is worth more than every leaderboard you will read this year.",
