      "heading": "The Den Den Mushi",
      "narration": "A Transponder Snail is a lovely piece of technology to think with, because everything true of it is true of an API call. When Luffy calls somebody on a Den Den Mushi, he gets access to knowledge that is not on the ship. He is talking to an expert who is somewhere else, who knows things no one aboard knows, and he did not have to feed that expert or find them a bunk. That is an enormous capability multiplier for a small crew, and it is exactly what a hosted API is: you are renting an ear and a mind that live on someone else's infrastructure. And notice the thing that is easy to miss and matters most: the capability is not the crew's, and it does not become the crew's. However many times Robin calls a scholar, the crew does not become scholars. Now the properties of the call, all of which the show is careful about. There is a delay — you wait for the connection and you wait for the answer, and in the middle of an emergency that delay is the whole problem. Someone can be listening; a line to the outside is a line the outside can listen to, and there are episodes that turn on exactly that. And the line can be cut, or the snail can be out of range, or the person on the other end can simply not pick up. Latency, privacy, availability. Those are not properties of the answer you get. They are properties of the fact that you asked somebody else. So the crew also carries a Baby Den Den Mushi, and it is the local model. Shorter range, less capability, no access to anyone outside — and it is on the ship, it works in the middle of the ocean, it costs nothing to use, and nobody else hears it. When you must have an answer and cannot rely on a line to the outside world, that is what you have, and that is exactly the argument for running a quantized model locally: it is not as good, and it is always there, and some of your requirements are about always-there rather than about best. Which brings me to the point of the picture. The skill Nami and Robin actually demonstrate is not owning the most powerful snail. It is judgement about which calls are worth making. Most questions on a ship are answered by asking the person standing next to you. You place a call when the question genuinely needs someone who is not aboard — and you place it knowing it will cost you time, and that you are telling somebody outside the crew what you are asking about. That is routing, and it is the same discipline. Ask the small model first. Reach for the line when the question actually needs it.",
